/*Try this out:
- ctrl+f7 toggles the status bar below for general info
- ctrl+alt+d for debug mode
- ctrl+alt+c to compile
- put a breakpoint next to "mov ax, 5" in left grey column with mouse left click
or f9 sets a breakpoint at the text cursor line
- run the code using f5 and step over each instruction with f10
- ctrl+shift+g to move between code and asm lines
- try putting registers and variables in the upper right watch window
ctrl+alt+o toggles text op messages in debug mode.
ctrl+alt+i toggles disassembly in debug mode.
ctrl+alt+1 text pane
ctrl+alt+2 watch pane
ctrl+alt+3 disassembly pane
*/
main :: () {
	/*Registers can be int (integer) or float (real number).
	Each register is 8 bytes (64 bits).
	Integers and floats are 8 bytes (64 bytes).
	All register names can be found in thegame_data.jai in the RegDef :: enum.
	Use ax, bx, cx, dx, r0, r1, r2, r3 for general assembly code.
	Other registers may be used by the compiler and cpu.
	*/
	#asm {
		mov ax, 5; //move integer 5 into register ax
		mov.f bx, 3.14159; //move real number 3.14159 into register bx
		mov cx, bx; //move register bx into register cx
	}

	/*Random-access memory (ram) is a sequence of bytes.
	1 byte == 8 bits and 8 bytes == 64 bits.
	Square brackets are used to represent ram values.
	So ram data at address 0 is [0].
	Ram can be accessed in the top-right watch pane with ram(0) or ram(x).

	address  data
	     24    12
	     16    12
	      8    1.618033
	      0    12
	*/
	#asm {
		mov [0], 12; //move integer 12 to [0]
		mov.f [8], 1.618033; //move real number 1.618033 to [8]
		//the third operand defaults to 8 if not set
		mov [16], [0], 8; //move [0] to [16] for 8 bytes
		mov ax, 24;
		//registers can be used to access ram
		mov [ax], [16], 8;
		//1 addition can be used as an offset for ram access
		mov [ax+8], [ax], 8;
	}

	/*sbp (stack base pointer) register can be used to place values 
	relative to the current function stack in memory.
	*/
	#asm {
		mov [sbp+0], 10, 8;
		mov [sbp+8], [sbp+0], 8;
	}
	/*Variables are stored on the stack by the compiler using the sbp register.
	Each value is put on top of the previous one like a physical stack:

	address    data
	 sbp+16      ...
	  sbp+8      10
	  sbp+0      10

	Variables on the stack are only accessible in this function.
	Compile with ctrl+alt+c to ensure code and asm match up.
	Press ctrl+shift+g to jump to the corresponding asm instruction below.
	The following is equivalent to the sbp asm above.
	*/
	x : int = 10;
	y : int = x;

	/*Each variable is represent by name and data type known at compile time.
	Variables are declared with 
	variable_name : data_type;
	x : int;

	A value can be set with
	variable_name : data_type = value;
	x : int = 10;

	The data type can be inferred by the value with 
	variable_name := value;
	x := 10;
	*/

	/*Each value in memory is associated to a corresponding memory address.
	Addresses can be accessed for any variable by the unary operator "*".
	Try putting px and *x in the watch window.
	*/
	px : *int = *x;
	py : *int = *y;

	/*To access a specific address' data, the address data operator ".*" can be used.
	Notice that this changes the data in x because px is the memory address to the data in x.
	*/
	px.* = 5;

	/*Heap memory is accessible outside of this function.
	The New function is used to get some heap memory by data type.
	New always returns an address to heap memory.
	*/
	h : *int = New(int);
	f : *float = New(float);

	/*To learn about arrays
	load_op arrays guides/programming/arrays
	*/

	/*To learn about conditionals
	load_op conditionals guides/programming/conditionals
	*/
}